  "initial_reflection": {
    "summary": "The run developed a reasonable early Ironclad deck and reached Hexaghost with strong HP, but it failed to convert that position into a win. The most important issues were giving up the Sapphire Key for Strike Dummy despite a possible Heart-oriented route, and repeatedly choosing damage over survival during the final combat when defensive cards were legal.",
    "lessons": [
      {
        "category": "BOSS_REWARD",
        "title": "Prioritize the Sapphire Key when pursuing a Heart run",
        "situation": "When a linked relic requires surrendering the Sapphire Key and the run is intended to pursue the Heart.",
        "lesson": "Do not trade away the Sapphire Key for a merely moderate relic such as Strike Dummy; take the key unless the run has deliberately abandoned the Heart objective.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "At Act 1 Floor 9, the choice was explicitly between Strike Dummy and the Sapphire Key, and Strike Dummy was chosen.",
          "The run ended with ruby=False, emerald=False, and sapphire=False."
        ],
        "reasoning": "The decision permanently removed the possibility of collecting all three keys in this run, while the resulting relic was not enough to prevent the later boss loss. The reusable behavior is to treat the Sapphire Key as the priority when the Heart is a goal.",
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "category": "COMBAT",
        "title": "Stop spending the last energy on damage when an attack is incoming",
        "situation": "During a boss turn, especially at low or moderate HP, after an attack card has already consumed most of the turn's energy.",
        "lesson": "After using a costly attack, use remaining energy on legal block rather than adding a small attack when the enemy is attacking and the extra damage cannot end the fight.",
        "evidence_points": [
          "On Turn 7 against Hexaghost, HP was 32, the enemy intent was ATTACK, and after Bash+ left 1 energy, both Shrug It Off and Defend were legal; the agent instead played Pommel Strike.",
          "The next logged state was Turn 8 at HP 15, showing that the intervening attack caused a major loss of survivability.",
          "On Turn 9 at HP 14, the agent again played Shrug It Off and then spent the remaining 2 energy on Bash+ against a 39-HP Hexaghost, leaving the boss alive."
        ],
        "reasoning": "The Turn 7 choice is a clear defensive opportunity: the attack did not finish Hexaghost, while a block card was available before an incoming attack. The subsequent HP collapse left little margin for the final turns. Exact incoming damage is not logged, so this is a high-confidence sequencing lesson rather than a precise damage counterfactual.",
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    ]
  },
  "review_decision": "HUMAN_TEACHING",
  "human_feedback": "Your deck-building decisions are too focused on whether each individual card is locally useful. Build toward a coherent long-term deck plan instead. Before adding a card, identify what the current deck's damage plan, defense plan, scaling, draw/energy needs, and major weaknesses are. Add a card only when it meaningfully fills one of those needs, strengthens an existing synergy, or enables a clear new strategy; otherwise skip it. Do not keep adding generically good cards simply because each one can be justified individually.\r\n\r\nIn combat, preserve the full-turn sequence you planned. If you plan to use setup followed by defense, do not abandon the defensive part on the next decision unless the game state materially changes. When lethal is not available and an enemy is attacking, avoid preventable HP loss when available block can substantially reduce or fully mitigate that damage and extra damage does not meaningfully change the fight. In particular, do not spend the remaining energy on marginal attacks after already planning to block.",
  "finalized_at": "2026-10-13T11:49:56.724599+00:00"
}